Rating guidelines: A company that practices proactive transparency across all categories by publishing comprehensive, audited disclosures, freely cooperates with journalists and researchers, provides open access to its supply chain and ownership data, and protects whistleblowers without restrictive NDAs gets a 100. A company that adheres strictly to standard statutory disclosure mandates (such as mandatory SEC filings) and basic public relations responses, but provides little voluntary transparency beyond legal minimums gets a 50. A company that operates in deliberate secrecy, conceals ownership through shell companies, aggressively stonewalls journalists, enforces gag orders and NDAs, retaliates against whistleblowers, or exhibits a suspicious absence of information across key ethical areas gets a 1.